// The revl stdlib RENDER module (roadmap item 195, option C).
//
// A Path B enabler, kin to stdlib/list.rvl (194) and stdlib/value.rvl (180):
// the blessed threaded-accumulator combinator a self-hosted emitter reaches for
// when it must thread a document-wide counter through a run of pure renderers.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the tax
// revl has NO emitter-local mutable state. The reference emitters keep the
// document-wide `$revl_match_N` temp counter on a mutable `_Ctx` and bump it in
// place; a pure revl port cannot, so the counter is threaded EXPLICITLY as
// `render(node, counter) -> {text, counter}` through every combinator in exact
// evaluation order. The threading itself is one shape repeated: seed a counter,
// walk a list left-to-right, and at each step feed the PREVIOUS step's counter
// into the next render, collecting each rendered fragment. Hand-rolled that is
//
//     var c = counter
//     var texts = []
//     for (e of nodes) {
//       let r = render(e, c)     // the previous `c`, in order
//       texts = texts.push(r.text)
//       c = r.counter            // drop this line and the name is silently wrong
//     }
//
// re-derived at every list-shaped site (call args, tuple/record fields, match
// arms). Option C keeps the purity model INTACT (no hidden mutable counter,
// nothing survives a pure-fn call unthreaded) and instead names that one shape
// once: `render_seq`. The typed `{text, counter}` cell is retained, so a dropped
// counter is still a COMPILE error (the accumulator is not optional), not a
// silent wrong `$revl_match_N`.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ pure revl
// Like stdlib/list.rvl (and unlike value.rvl), every function here is PURE revl
// built on the base List surface (`for … of`, `.push`). There is no `@py` block,
// so there is nothing to defer per tier: the module compiles and runs
// identically on EVERY backend the moment they emit the base List builtins. The
// py tier is executed by the test suite (tests/test_render_stdlib.py); the other
// tiers inherit for free.
//
// ------------------------------------------------ evaluation order is the point
// `render_seq` threads the counter STRICTLY left-to-right: item i is rendered
// with the counter that item i-1 returned, and the returned counter is the one
// the LAST item left. This is the whole correctness property (the emitter's
// `$revl_match_N` numbering is defined by evaluation order), so it is pinned
// directly in tests/test_render_stdlib.py: a renderer that stamps the counter it
// was given reproduces the seed sequence exactly, and the final counter equals
// `seed + len`.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------- {text, counter}, not S
// `render_seq` is specialised to the `Rendered = {text, counter}` cell rather
// than a generic `{value: V, state: S}`, for the SAME reason stdlib/list.rvl is
// `Str`-specialised: revl's generics do not carry the shape cleanly across the
// boundary a caller needs. A generic `render_seq[E, S]` type-checks in isolation,
// but the renderer a caller passes returns the caller's OWN nominal cell (the
// emitter's `Rendered`), and two nominal records unify only by NAME, so a
// generic `{value, state}` param rejects it; and the generic RETURN does not
// concretise at the call site, so `r.texts` lands unpinned. Owning ONE cell type
// that the caller imports sidesteps both: the counter (an `Int`) IS the threaded
// accumulator, and anything a caller wants to thread it encodes into that `Int`.
// The element type `E` stays generic, so the sequence walks a list of anything
// (the emitter threads over `Any` IR nodes). A general `{value, state}` combinator
// is a language/generics question, not stdlib; see the item 195 note.
//
// Import with a named `use`:
//
//     use "stdlib/render.rvl" { render_seq, Rendered, Threaded }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the cell
// One rendered fragment AND the running counter threaded past it. The counter is
// the state a caller carries in evaluation order (the emitter's document-wide
// `$revl_match_N` gensym); a caller's own render fn returns this shape, so it is
// exported for the caller to name in its signatures.
pub type Rendered = { text: Str, counter: Int }

// The result of threading a whole sequence: every fragment in order AND the
// counter the last render left. `texts` is joined/wrapped by the caller (the
// combinator does not assume a separator), `counter` is threaded onward.
pub type Threaded = { texts: List[Str], counter: Int }

// -------------------------------------------------------------- the combinator
// Thread `counter` left-to-right through `render` over each item, collecting the
// rendered fragments. `render` is called with the item and the counter the
// PREVIOUS item returned (the first with `counter` itself); the counter it
// returns is fed to the next and, from the last item, becomes the result's
// `counter`. This is the one blessed shape: a caller writes the sequence as a
// single call instead of re-rolling the seed/loop/hand-back by hand.
pub fn render_seq[E](items: List[E], counter: Int, render: (E, Int) -> Rendered) -> Threaded {
  var c = counter
  var texts = []
  for (e of items) {
    let r = render(e, c)
    texts = texts.push(r.text)
    c = r.counter
  }
  return { texts: texts, counter: c }
}
